  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1200 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nUp to Thursday night, no weather influence from developing tropical \ndepression 9 should be noticeable over South Central TX. Based on \nthe robust strengthening forecast by the NHC over the next couple \ndays would have us watching a hurricane over the central Gulf \nheading northeast. The small-ish system and the tracking direction \naway from TX has the model data showing almost no wind impact for \nour forecast area, with maybe just a hint we'll see the surface \nwinds become more northerly in direction Friday afternoon. However, \nthose with weekend plans for the coast will want to stay in touch \nwith our sister NWS Offices in CRP/HGX/BRO to know about the risk of \nhigher tides and rip currents.  \n\nBy Friday night we should be fully removed from any future impacts \nof the Gulf tropical system, assuming the track and landfall \nprojections are within the cone of uncertainty. The weakness that \nmoved into West TX disappears and the weekend will see the \nsubtropical ridge build slightly over TX. However, there is another \nweakness patter developing to the west but further out. The Eastern \nPacific cyclone Rachael, currently still a hurricane, will weaken \nand get absorbed into a extratropical trough, and another developing \ntropical low is projected to take shape and work its way NW along \nthe southern coast of Mexico. These approaching weaknesses should \nreveal little impact over TX Saturday, but could begin impacting the \narea Sunday, mainly through a strengthening onshore wind. By Monday \nmorning low, temperatures and humidity levels will take a \nsignificant turn upward, giving us that sticky October air that we \nare accustomed to. Most of the model solutions favor enough moisture \nmoving inland to set up some diurnal convection. This tropical air \nmass signal looks to get more traction going well into next week as \nthe upper trough and eventual cold front draw closer.",
